[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Testing Vue Applications with Vitest, Vue Test Utils, and Cypress"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"Testing is an essential part of modern Vue.js development. A robust testing strategy ensures your components render correctly, react to user input as expected, and maintain stability as your application scales. In this tutorial, we will explore the modern Vue testing stack: Vitest for unit testing, Vue Test Utils for mounting and interacting with components, and Cypress for end-to-end (E2E) testing."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"1. Setting Up Vitest and Vue Test Utils"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"Vitest is a blazing fast unit test framework powered by Vite. When creating a new Vue project via create-vue, you can include Vitest automatically. If you are adding it manually, install the required packages using your package manager."},{"order":5,"type":"code","text":"npm install --save-dev vitest @vue/test-utils jsdom"},{"order":6,"type":"paragraph","text":"Next, configure your vite.config.js file to include the test block so Vitest knows how to handle component mounting in a simulated DOM environment."},{"order":7,"type":"code","text":"import { defineConfig } from 'vite';
import vue from '@vitejs/plugin-vue';

export default defineConfig({
  plugins: [vue()],
  test: {
    environment: 'jsdom',
  },
});"},{"order":8,"type":"subheading","text":"2. Writing Your First Component Unit Test"},{"order":9,"type":"paragraph","text":"Let us consider a simple Counter.vue component that increments a value when a button is clicked. We will write a unit test to verify this behavior using Vue Test Utils and Vitest."},{"order":10,"type":"code","text":"import { mount } from '@vue/test-utils';
import { describe, it, expect } from 'vitest';
import Counter from '@/components/Counter.vue';

describe('Counter.vue', () => {
  it('renders properly and increments value on click', async () => {
    const wrapper = mount(Counter);

    // Verify initial state
    expect(wrapper.text()).toContain('Count: 0');

    // Simulate user interaction
    await wrapper.find('button').trigger('click');

    // Verify updated state
    expect(wrapper.text()).toContain('Count: 1');
  });
});"},{"order":11,"type":"subheading","text":"3. Introduction to End-to-End (E2E) Testing with Cypress"},{"order":12,"type":"paragraph","text":"While unit tests verify individual components in isolation, E2E tests validate entire user workflows across the fully rendered application in a real browser. Cypress is the industry standard for Vue E2E testing."},{"order":13,"type":"code","text":"npm install --save-dev cypress"},{"order":14,"type":"paragraph","text":"Open the Cypress Test Runner using the following command:"},{"order":15,"type":"code","text":"npx cypress open"},{"order":16,"type":"subheading","text":"4. Writing an E2E Test in Cypress"},{"order":17,"type":"paragraph","text":"Create an E2E test file (e.g., counter.cy.js) inside your Cypress integration folder to test the complete user journey from navigating to the page to interacting with the counter feature."},{"order":18,"type":"code","text":"describe('Counter E2E Test', () => {
  it('visits the app root and interacts with the counter', () => {
    cy.visit('/');
    cy.get('h1').should('contain', 'Welcome to Your Vue.js App');

    // Click the increment button twice
    cy.get('[data-testid="increment-btn"]').click();
    cy.get('[data-testid="increment-btn"]').click();

    // Assert the final count displayed on the page
    cy.get('[data-testid="count-display"]').should('have.text', '2');
  });
});"},{"order":19,"type":"subheading","text":"Conclusion and Best Practices"},{"order":20,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Use Vitest and Vue Test Utils for fast, isolated verification of component logic, props, and emitted events."},{"order":21,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Use Cypress to test critical paths, routing, and integration with backend APIs."},{"order":22,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Adopt a testing pyramid approach: write many unit tests, a moderate amount of integration tests, and essential E2E tests."}]
